{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SGGWbKWsQpzEvS5GNdcSxZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/quic.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! `quic.h` is the kitchen-sink public header for the\n4\t//! quic-core library: it defines the protocol error codes, the\n5\t//! transport-parameter identifiers, the application-facing enums, the\n6\t//! opaque QUIC / connection / path types, the public structs that\n7\t//! cross the API boundary (`TransportParameters`, `PathQuality`,\n8\t//! `PerAckState`, `CongestionAlgorithm`, …)\n9\t//! and the dozens of free functions that make up the application API.\n10\t//!\n11\t//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every body is `todo!()`.\n12\t//!\n13\t//! Pointer-shape and translation policy notes that apply throughout\n14\t//! this module:\n15\t//!\n16\t//! * `Quic`, `Connection`, `Path` are\n17\t//!   opaque types defined in `internal.h`.  Phase 1 declares\n18\t//!   them as empty structs here so the public API can refer to them;\n19\t//!   the real layout lands when the internal header is translated.\n20\t//! * `struct sockaddr*` parameters and fields map to\n21\t//!   [`core::net::SocketAddr`].  The C source uses tagged unions of\n22\t//!   `sockaddr_in` / `sockaddr_in6`, which `SocketAddr` models\n23\t//!   directly.  Output parameters of type `struct sockaddr_storage*`\n24\t//!   become a `SocketAddr` returned by value.\n25\t//! * Function-pointer typedefs become traits (one trait per typedef\n26\t//!   by default).  Trait grouping is used for the four-function\n27\t//!   `CongestionAlgorithm` vtable, where the C side\n28\t//!   always installs the four together.\n29\t//! * `void*` \"callback context\" arguments are folded into the trait\n30\t//!   implementor's state.  Per-stream / per-path application\n31\t//!   contexts that flow through the stack (e.g. `stream_ctx`,\n32\t//!   `app_stream_ctx`) become `Option<Box<dyn core::any::Any>>` —\n33\t//!   the application produces them, downcasts on retrieval.\n34\t//! * C `int` / `int64_t` return values that encode 0/-1 or 0/error\n35\t//!   status become `Result<T, Error>` — the crate-level `Error` enum\n36\t//!   doesn't exist yet, so `()` is a placeholder per the Phase 1\n37\t//!   contract.  `i32` / `i64` are kept where the C value is a real\n38\t//!   integer (e.g., wake delays in microseconds, interface index).\n39\t//! * Single-bit `unsigned int : 1` bitfields collapse to `bool`.\n40\t//! * `va_list` has no Rust counterpart; the `_v` log helpers are\n41\t//!   omitted in favour of one entry point that takes\n42\t//!   `core::fmt::Arguments<'_>`.\n43\t//! * `extern` global state (`congestion_control_algorithms`)\n44\t//!   becomes accessor functions that hand out a borrowed slice; the\n45\t//!   companion `_nb_` length variable disappears (the slice carries\n46\t//!   its length).\n47\t\n48\t// Many translated functions mirror C signatures with >7 parameters.\n49\t// Builder patterns or shape changes are out of scope for Phase 1.\n50\t#![allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]\n51\t\n52\tpub mod arena;\n53\tpub mod binlog;\n54\tpub mod bytestream;\n55\tpub mod cc_common;\n56\tpub mod config;\n57\tpub mod crypto;\n58\tpub mod errors;\n59\tpub mod frames;\n60\tpub mod hash;\n61\tpub mod header_protection;\n62\tpub mod internal;\n63\tpub mod lb;\n64\tpub mod logger;\n65\tpub mod packet_loop;\n66\tpub mod performance_log;\n67\tpub mod qlog;\n68\tpub mod siphash;\n69\tpub mod socks;\n70\tpub mod socks_socket2;\n71\tpub mod splay;\n72\tpub mod stream;\n73\tpub mod sys;\n74\t#[cfg(test)]\n75\tpub mod tests;\n76\tpub mod textlog;\n77\tpub mod tls;\n78\tpub mod tls_api;\n79\tpub mod tp;\n80\tpub mod utils;\n81\t\n82\t// Re-export the TP shapes from the public crate root for backwards\n83\t// compatibility with callers that already wrote `crate::TransportParameters`.\n84\tpub use tp::{PreferredAddress, TransportParameter, TransportParameters, VersionNegotiation};\n85\t\n86\tuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n87\t\n88\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n89\t// Version string and error class.\n90\t//\n91\t// Error values come straight from `#define` and would naturally be\n92\t// untyped in C; in Rust we commit to `u64` because the application\n93\t// API takes them as `uint64_t error_code` parameters and returns\n94\t// them in `get_local_error` etc.\n95\t\n96\t/// Library version string.  C: `VERSION`.\n97\tpub const VERSION: &str = \"1.1.48.0\";\n98\t\n99\t/// Crate-level error type for fallible operations.\n100\t///\n101\t/// Phase 1 stubs return `Result<T, Error>` even though the\n102\t/// `todo!()` bodies don't yet decide which variant to produce —\n103\t/// Phase 3 will fill that in based on the C control flow.  The\n104\t/// initial variant set covers the broad failure shapes; Phase 3\n105\t/// can add more as needed.  Marked `#[non_exhaustive]` so adding\n106\t/// variants later isn't a breaking change.\n107\t#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n108\t#[non_exhaustive]\n109\tpub enum Error {\n110\t    /// Catch-all placeholder for paths Phase 1 has not yet\n111\t    /// distinguished.  Phase 3 should narrow these.\n112\t    Generic,\n113\t    /// Allocation failed.\n114\t    Memory,\n115\t    /// Caller supplied an invalid argument.\n116\t    InvalidArgument,\n117\t    /// A buffer the caller supplied was too small.\n118\t    BufferTooSmall,\n119\t    /// Caller-supplied file does not exist.\n120\t    NoSuchFile,\n121\t    /// Caller-supplied file was malformed.\n122\t    InvalidFile,\n123\t    /// Connection has been disconnected.\n124\t    Disconnected,\n125\t    /// Connection state didn't permit the requested operation.\n126\t    InvalidState,\n127\t    /// Operation produced or received a malformed QUIC frame.\n128\t    InvalidFrame,\n129\t    /// TLS / crypto error.\n130\t    Tls,\n131\t    /// Other QUIC protocol error; the wrapped value is the\n132\t    /// numeric `ERROR_*` code defined in this module.\n133\t    Protocol(u64),\n134\t}\n135\t\n136\timpl core::fmt::Display for Error {\n137\t    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut core::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> core::fmt::Result {\n138\t        write!(f, \"{:?}\", self)\n139\t    }\n140\t}\n141\t\n142\timpl core::error::Error for Error {}\n143\t\n144\t/// Crate-wide `Result` alias defaulting the error type to [`Error`].\n145\tpub type Result<T, E = Error> = core::result::Result<T, E>;\n146\t\n147\t/// Wall-clock instant in microseconds since some application-defined\n148\t/// epoch.  Replaces the C `uint64_t current_time` parameter that\n149\t/// threads through every API call needing \"now\".  The type is\n150\t/// `Copy`, supports arithmetic with [`Duration`], and converts to"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"513641af-66d9-4e8b-98cd-e59b8e6a1fb7","uuid":"19c4ce15-df3a-4e79-9433-ac1987d2bae7","timestamp":"2026-05-04T22:22:18.748Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/lib.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/quic.h`.\n//!\n//! `quic.h` is the kitchen-sink public header for the\n//! quic-core library: it defines the protocol error codes, the\n//! transport-parameter identifiers, the application-facing enums, the\n//! opaque QUIC / connection / path types, the public structs that\n//! cross the API boundary (`TransportParameters`, `PathQuality`,\n//! `PerAckState`, `CongestionAlgorithm`, …)\n//! and the dozens of free functions that make up the application API.\n//!\n//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every body is `todo!()`.\n//!\n//! Pointer-shape and translation policy notes that apply throughout\n//! this module:\n//!\n//! * `Quic`, `Connection`, `Path` are\n//!   opaque types defined in `internal.h`.  Phase 1 declares\n//!   them as empty structs here so the public API can refer to them;\n//!   the real layout lands when the internal header is translated.\n//! * `struct sockaddr*` parameters and fields map to\n//!   [`core::net::SocketAddr`].  The C source uses tagged unions of\n//!   `sockaddr_in` / `sockaddr_in6`, which `SocketAddr` models\n//!   directly.  Output parameters of type `struct sockaddr_storage*`\n//!   become a `SocketAddr` returned by value.\n//! * Function-pointer typedefs become traits (one trait per typedef\n//!   by default).  Trait grouping is used for the four-function\n//!   `CongestionAlgorithm` vtable, where the C side\n//!   always installs the four together.\n//! * `void*` \"callback context\" arguments are folded into the trait\n//!   implementor's state.  Per-stream / per-path application\n//!   contexts that flow through the stack (e.g. `stream_ctx`,\n//!   `app_stream_ctx`) become `Option<Box<dyn core::any::Any>>` —\n//!   the application produces them, downcasts on retrieval.\n//! * C `int` / `int64_t` return values that encode 0/-1 or 0/error\n//!   status become `Result<T, Error>` — the crate-level `Error` enum\n//!   doesn't exist yet, so `()` is a placeholder per the Phase 1\n//!   contract.  `i32` / `i64` are kept where the C value is a real\n//!   integer (e.g., wake delays in microseconds, interface index).\n//! * Single-bit `unsigned int : 1` bitfields collapse to `bool`.\n//! * `va_list` has no Rust counterpart; the `_v` log helpers are\n//!   omitted in favour of one entry point that takes\n//!   `core::fmt::Arguments<'_>`.\n//! * `extern` global state (`congestion_control_algorithms`)\n//!   becomes accessor functions that hand out a borrowed slice; the\n//!   companion `_nb_` length variable disappears (the slice carries\n//!   its length).\n\n// Many translated functions mirror C signatures with >7 parameters.\n// Builder patterns or shape changes are out of scope for Phase 1.\n#![allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]\n\npub mod arena;\npub mod binlog;\npub mod bytestream;\npub mod cc_common;\npub mod config;\npub mod crypto;\npub mod errors;\npub mod frames;\npub mod hash;\npub mod header_protection;\npub mod internal;\npub mod lb;\npub mod logger;\npub mod packet_loop;\npub mod performance_log;\npub mod qlog;\npub mod siphash;\npub mod socks;\npub mod socks_socket2;\npub mod splay;\npub mod stream;\npub mod sys;\n#[cfg(test)]\npub mod tests;\npub mod textlog;\npub mod tls;\npub mod tls_api;\npub mod tp;\npub mod utils;\n\n// Re-export the TP shapes from the public crate root for backwards\n// compatibility with callers that already wrote `crate::TransportParameters`.\npub use tp::{PreferredAddress, TransportParameter, TransportParameters, VersionNegotiation};\n\nuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Version string and error class.\n//\n// Error values come straight from `#define` and would naturally be\n// untyped in C; in Rust we commit to `u64` because the application\n// API takes them as `uint64_t error_code` parameters and returns\n// them in `get_local_error` etc.\n\n/// Library version string.  C: `VERSION`.\npub const VERSION: &str = \"1.1.48.0\";\n\n/// Crate-level error type for fallible operations.\n///\n/// Phase 1 stubs return `Result<T, Error>` even though the\n/// `todo!()` bodies don't yet decide which variant to produce —\n/// Phase 3 will fill that in based on the C control flow.  The\n/// initial variant set covers the broad failure shapes; Phase 3\n/// can add more as needed.  Marked `#[non_exhaustive]` so adding\n/// variants later isn't a breaking change.\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub enum Error {\n    /// Catch-all placeholder for paths Phase 1 has not yet\n    /// distinguished.  Phase 3 should narrow these.\n    Generic,\n    /// Allocation failed.\n    Memory,\n    /// Caller supplied an invalid argument.\n    InvalidArgument,\n    /// A buffer the caller supplied was too small.\n    BufferTooSmall,\n    /// Caller-supplied file does not exist.\n    NoSuchFile,\n    /// Caller-supplied file was malformed.\n    InvalidFile,\n    /// Connection has been disconnected.\n    Disconnected,\n    /// Connection state didn't permit the requested operation.\n    InvalidState,\n    /// Operation produced or received a malformed QUIC frame.\n    InvalidFrame,\n    /// TLS / crypto error.\n    Tls,\n    /// Other QUIC protocol error; the wrapped value is the\n    /// numeric `ERROR_*` code defined in this module.\n    Protocol(u64),\n}\n\nimpl core::fmt::Display for Error {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut core::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> core::fmt::Result {\n        write!(f, \"{:?}\", self)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl core::error::Error for Error {}\n\n/// Crate-wide `Result` alias defaulting the error type to [`Error`].\npub type Result<T, E = Error> = core::result::Result<T, E>;\n\n/// Wall-clock instant in microseconds since some application-defined\n/// epoch.  Replaces the C `uint64_t current_time` parameter that\n/// threads through every API call needing \"now\".  The type is\n/// `Copy`, supports arithmetic with [`Duration`], and converts to","numLines":150,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2425}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_018REGSGY9wfQn5tHYsJM7ZQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good understanding of the codebase. Let me look at what API methods exist on `Quic` and `Connection` to understand what I need to translate the cnxstress tests.\n\nLooking at the C code, let me analyze the two tests I need to translate:\n\n1. `cnx_limit_test` - Tests that creating more connections than the limit fails. Uses `cnx_stress_create_ctx`, `cnx_stress_loop_step`, `cnx_stress_delete_ctx`.\n\n2. `cnx_stress_unit_test` - Just calls `cnx_stress_do_test(120000000, 100, 0)`.\n   \nBoth tests depend on a stress testing framework with a context structure, a simulation loop, and initialization functions that set up Quic contexts and simulated links. The core pattern is creating a stress context, running the simulation loop until completion, then verifying the results.\n\nThe second test specifically validates that when the server reaches its client limit, it properly rejects additional connections with a server_busy response. The challenge is that the C implementation spans hundreds of lines of infrastructure—connection callbacks, link simulation, state management—that would need faithful translation to Rust. I need to decide what's practical to port directly versus what can be simplified while still testing the same behavior. to pass, just compile\n- `todo!()` panics in API bodies are fine\n- I can add `todo!()` stubs for missing API methods\n\nThe key challenge here is that `cnxstress.c` is a very large test with complex infrastructure. The tests themselves are simple wrappers, but they depend on the entire `cnx_stress_ctx_t` infrastructure. The right approach is to port all the necessary infrastructure—data structures and functions—into `cnxstress.rs`, then use that in the test bodies.\n\nNow I'm identifying which API methods I need from the Rust side: the core QUIC operations like creating contexts and connections, starting clients, closing connections, and managing simulation links for packet handling. I also need various utility methods for preparing packets, processing incoming data, managing wake times, and configuring transport parameters.\n\nMany of these are likely stubs or don't exist yet, so I'll need to add them. For the callback mechanism, I'll use Rust's `StreamDataCallback` trait instead of C function pointers. My plan is to define the context structs, implement the callback trait, and then build out the stress test infrastructure.\n\nThis is a substantial amount of code to translate from the C file, so I need to carefully map out what public API methods are missing. Let me review what I need to add to the library.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10748,"cache_read_input_tokens":43414,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10748},"output_tokens":31,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"513641af-66d9-4e8b-98cd-e59b8e6a1fb7","uuid":"c40f1229-4555-4d12-b72a-087d53ea1784"}
