{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Lderd32fadDT4torKUtG69","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, `TransportError::ServerBusy = 0x2` is the right one. Now I have all the information I need to write the translation.\n\nLet me also check what methods I need for the `TestSimLink` and `TestSimPacket`:\n\n1. `TestSimLink::next_arrival` - exists (returns u64, not Instant)\n2. `TestSimLink.packets` - the deque of packets, I need to check the first element's arrival time\n3. `TestSimLink::dequeue` - exists (returns `Option<TestSimPacket>`)\n4. `TestSimLink::submit` - exists\n\n\n5. `TestSimLink::dequeue` - I need to call this with the current time to get the next packet, then process it by calling `incoming_packet` on the quic connection with the packet's bytes and address information\n\n.\n\n6. The key issue is that `incoming_packet` expects a mutable slice of bytes, but I'm getting an immutable packet from `dequeue`. However, since `dequeue` returns the packet by value, I own it and can create a mutable reference to its byte buffer. So I can bind the result to a mutable variable and then take a mutable slice of the bytes array up to the packet's length.\n\nFor the outgoing packet side, I'm looking at how the C code creates a packet, calls `prepare_next_packet` to fill it, and then submits it to the link if anything was prepared. I need to translate this same pattern to Rust.\n\nNow I'm thinking about how to handle the address family check — in C it's checking if `ss_family == AF_UNSPEC`, but in Rust I need to figure out what represents an unspecified address. Looking at the `PreparedPacket` structure...\n\nDefining the packet structure... default_source as the fallback when the address family is unspecified. Since the simulation doesn't need exact address verification, I can just use a sensible default like `0.0.0.0:0` or always substitute the default source address when the port is zero.\n\nNow I'm ready to start writing the actual Rust implementation, beginning with the main file structure. I also need to think through the `Quic::connection_by_id` stub and how it'll work with the connection stress test message handling.\n\nLooking at the C code, the limit test iterates through client contexts and checks if each connection has reached the `ClientAlmostReady` state by accessing the connection object. I'll need to implement `connection_by_id` to retrieve connections so I can check their state in the Rust equivalent.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how to handle `simulated_time` — in the C version it's stored in the stress context and updated in the main loop, while callbacks access it through the connection's quic object. In Rust, I could get the time directly from the connection, but I need to figure out the cleanest way to pass that through the callback chain. Writing the Rust translation... Finishing up the function signatures for the stress test suite, then I need to look at what the TransportParameters API expects so I can properly set the default transport parameters.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":879,"cache_read_input_tokens":92786,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":879},"output_tokens":32,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"513641af-66d9-4e8b-98cd-e59b8e6a1fb7","uuid":"44f5201f-ee17-4b79-a9be-87af94a588d4"}
